        // ---- the star band: what a rarity is worth, measured -----------------------------------

        /// <summary>The two edges of the band, as a fraction of the role's authored 5-star
        /// median.</summary>
        private const float StarBandFloor = 0.45f;
        private const float StarBandCeiling = 0.85f;

        /// <summary>
        /// THE SAME TWO EDGES ONE TIER DOWN, and they are lower rather than narrower.
        ///
        /// A 3-star is authored with a basic, an active and one passive, which is the 4-star's
        /// shape minus whatever the 4-star spent its overflow slot on, so the tier below reads
        /// smaller for the same reason the tier above reads smaller than five: fewer rules, and
        /// a stat line that is a smaller fraction of its role's mean. The band was set where the
        /// tier actually lands once every 3-star has a real kit, not guessed in advance.
        /// </summary>
        private const float StarBandThreeFloor = 0.30f;
        private const float StarBandThreeCeiling = 0.60f;

        /// <summary>
        /// THE LAST TWO RUNGS, AND THE RULE THAT PICKED THEM.
        ///
        /// One rule builds the whole ladder, and it was read off the three tiers that were
        /// already authored rather than invented for these two: A TIER'S CEILING IS THE MEASURED
        /// MEDIAN OF THE TIER ABOVE IT, AND ITS FLOOR IS HALF ITS CEILING. Check it against what
        /// was already here - the sixteen graded 4-stars read a median of x0.61 and the 3-star
        /// ceiling is x0.60; the 4-star floor is 0.53 of its ceiling and the 3-star floor is
        /// exactly 0.50 of its. So:
        ///
        ///   two stars : the thirteen graded 3-stars read a median of x0.365, so the ceiling is
        ///               x0.36 - under it, which is what "sits below the tier above's middle"
        ///               means - and the floor is x0.18.
        ///   one star  : the twelve 2-stars read a median of x0.205, so the ceiling is x0.20 and
        ///               the floor is x0.10.
        ///
        /// The ladder of ceilings is 0.85, 0.60, 0.36, 0.20, which is a step of x0.71, x0.60 and
        /// x0.56 - the step widens as the tier falls, and that is the shape of the roster rather
        /// than a choice: three stars to two is the rung where the ACTIVE SKILL disappears, and
        /// two stars to one is where the passive does. A tier that loses a whole ability loses
        /// more than a tier that loses a clause.
        ///
        /// WHY THESE TWO TIERS READ SO MUCH SMALLER THAN THEIR ABILITY NUMBERS SUGGEST. A 2-star
        /// is a basic and a passive, and the sweep arena's straggler-and-four-walls line lasts
        /// long enough that a hero with no active spends every turn on one ability. It is also
        /// carrying 0.64 of its role's 5-star stat total at two stars and 0.52 at one, so it
        /// takes fewer turns before it dies. Both push the same way, and the authored damage
        /// numbers climb to answer them: the shipped basics run to about 350% of Attack at two
        /// stars against 251% on a 3-star and 110% on a 5-star. That is the tier ladder the
        /// roster already had - Pinoy wrote 105% to 110% at every tier and the band moved every
        /// one of them below five stars - and it is not new here.
        /// </summary>
        private const float StarBandTwoFloor = 0.18f;
        private const float StarBandTwoCeiling = 0.36f;
        private const float StarBandOneFloor = 0.10f;
        private const float StarBandOneCeiling = 0.20f;

        /// <summary>How many authored 5-stars a role needs before its median is treated as a
        /// middle rather than a sample, and the band's floor enforced against it.</summary>
        private const int StarBandMinFives = 3;

        /// <summary>
        /// HOW FAR A 5-STAR MAY SIT FROM ITS OWN ROLE'S 5-STAR MEDIAN: x0.78 to x1.64.
        ///
        /// THE 5-STARS ARE THE YARDSTICK, AND NOTHING WATCHED THE YARDSTICK. Every rung below five
        /// is graded against its role's 5-star median, and the 5-stars themselves were graded by
        /// nothing, so a 5-star could move 44% - Marrow did, in ENGINE-GAPS pass 2 - and redefine
        /// "in band" for every hero under it without a single check going red. Marrow's move
        /// happened to be toward his median rather than across it, which is why the median did not
        /// move; nothing in the harness would have said so either way.
        ///
        /// ONE TIER STEP IS x0.61, measured rather than chosen: it is the graded 4-stars' median
        /// against their 5-stars, and the 3-star and 2-star rungs sit at x0.60 and x0.62 of the
        /// rung above. The two edges are read off that step and are deliberately not symmetric,
        /// because the two directions do different damage.
        ///
        /// THE FLOOR IS HALF A STEP DOWN, sqrt(0.61) = x0.78. Below it a 5-star reads closer to the
        /// middle of its role's 4-stars than to the middle of its own tier, which breaks the one
        /// promise a star count makes a player - that a higher star is a bigger hero - and in the
        /// case that set this line, Aminah read x0.50 and sat under two of her role's three 4-stars.
        /// A low 5-star also threatens the yardstick directly: in a role of two or four, the median
        /// is the mean of the middle two, so one low reading drags every lower tier's grade with it.
        ///
        /// THE CEILING IS A WHOLE STEP UP, 1/0.61 = x1.64. A 5-star above its median breaks no
        /// ordering and moves no median unless it crosses the middle, which the floor above and the
        /// hero-sweep fingerprints already watch; the harm is balance inside the tier, and the
        /// tier's own unit of "a different class of hero" is a whole step. Half a step up would
        /// have failed three kits (Cyrra x1.37, Darian x1.48, Edrin x1.53) whose readings are
        /// printed here every run and named in DESIGN-BUILD-ORDER.md as the nearest to this edge.
        ///
        /// Graded in every role with two or more authored 5-stars. A role of one is its own median
        /// and is printed, not graded.
        /// </summary>
        private const float FiveStarFloor = 0.78f;
        private const float FiveStarCeiling = 1.64f;

        /// <summary>
        /// F8: A RARER ASPECT'S 5-STAR SITS AT OR ABOVE ITS ROLE'S MEDIAN, x1.00 to the same x1.64
        /// ceiling. The rarer aspects are the ones whose conversions take a seed
        /// (balance.json conversionSeedAspects, derived by CheckFusionEconomyDerivation): their
        /// fodder pools are four heroes against fourteen, so their 5-stars are the slow ones to
        /// fuse, and rarity with no payoff is a pool players learn to avoid. The other four aspects
        /// keep the whole x0.78 to x1.64 band. Reading the list off the recipe rather than naming
        /// two aspects here means the payoff follows the rarity if the rarity ever moves.
        /// </summary>
        private const float RareAspectFiveStarFloor = 1.00f;

        /// <summary>
        /// Rarer-aspect 5-stars F8's floor is not yet enforced on, each with why. The check fails if
        /// one reaches the floor (the pin is stale) and if anybody else falls under it. EMPTY.
        ///
        /// It held Velis, and the reason is worth keeping because it is a property of any role with
        /// an even count. The Striker role has four 5-stars, so its median is the mean of the middle
        /// two, and Velis at x0.91 could only reach it by entering that pair - which moved the
        /// median with him: his smallest clearing lift (x1.30 on his Damage) read x1.01 and took
        /// Kiva from x0.79 to x0.72. A hero inside the middle pair drags the yardstick he is graded
        /// against. So the two were moved together, and each lift carries its hero OUT of the
        /// middle pair: Velis above it (46,710 against Sylven's 44,379 and Vaelis's 45,505) and Kiva
        /// still below it. With both outside the pair the median is Sylven and Vaelis alone, 44,942,
        /// and neither hero can move it again: Velis reads x1.04 and Kiva x0.83.
        ///
        /// Kiva took the uniform step on every Damage number in her kit that Aminah and Caldus had,
        /// x1.15. Velis could not: the same step on his whole kit, x1.35, read x1.05 and put three of
        /// his clauses dark in THE ARENA MATRIX (talent I, What Was Taken and its remnant), because a
        /// harder Hollow the Wound kills before its Ledger reaches What Was Taken's threshold. His
        /// basic alone, Denied Return, took x1.45 and darkened nothing. x1.40 read 45,595, ninety
        /// power above Vaelis, which is a lift sitting on the median's edge rather than past it.
        /// </summary>
        private static readonly Dictionary<string, string> KnownF8Exceptions = new Dictionary<string, string>();

        private static float FiveStarFloorFor(ContentDatabase db, HeroDef def)
        {
            string[] seeds = db.Balance.ConversionSeedAspects;
            if (seeds != null)
                for (int i = 0; i < seeds.Length; i++)
                    if (seeds[i] == def.Aspect.ToString()) return RareAspectFiveStarFloor;
            return FiveStarFloor;
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// A 4-STAR HAS TO SIT BELOW THE 5-STARS OF ITS OWN ROLE, AND UNTIL NOW NOTHING SAID SO.
        ///
        /// The star contract is a shape rule - basic, active, passive1, passive2 where the design
        /// asks for it, three talents only at five - and `validate_heroes.py` enforces exactly that
        /// shape. Shape is not power: a 4-star authored with a 300% active and two riders satisfies
        /// every shape check in the project and is a better hero than the 5-star beside it. That
        /// gap is what this closes, and it is about to matter twenty-six times over, because the
        /// 4-star tier is being authored a batch at a time against 5-stars that are already fixed.
        ///
        /// ONE NUMBER, AND IT IS THE HERO'S OWN OUTPUT, not the battle's. Damage the subject dealt,
        /// plus healing it paid out, plus shielding it granted, summed over the hero sweep's five
        /// seeds in the hero sweep's arena - the same arena, the same squad, the same seeds, so a
        /// line here and a fingerprint there are two readings of one battle. Credited by SOURCE, so
        /// the four neutral fillers standing next to every subject contribute nothing to anybody's
        /// score and the number is a property of the kit rather than of the run.
        ///
        /// THE THREE TERMS ARE ADDED RATHER THAN RANKED SEPARATELY, and that is the compromise this
        /// instrument makes on purpose. A Mender's number is mostly healing and a Striker's is
        /// mostly damage, so the sum is not a cross-role currency and must not be read as one - it
        /// is only ever compared WITHIN a role, against the median of that role's authored 5-stars.
        /// Three columns per role would be more honest about what each hero does and could not
        /// answer the one question the tier is authored against, which is whether this hero is
        /// smaller than the ones above it.
        ///
        /// THE MEDIAN, NOT THE MINIMUM. A role's 5-stars are not equal - the sweep arena lights
        /// some kits and starves others - so the weakest of them is a number about the arena as
        /// much as about the hero. The median is what "the 5-stars of this role" means when there
        /// are several of them, and with one it is that one.
        ///
        /// A CEILING ALONE DOES NOT DESCRIBE A TIER. The first shape of this asserted one thing -
        /// a 4-star reads below its role's 5-star median - and a hero at x0.12 of that median
        /// passed it as comfortably as one at x0.84. "Smaller" was the whole claim, and a tier
        /// authored to be smaller with nothing saying HOW MUCH smaller is a tier of heroes nobody
        /// would field. So the band has two edges: every graded 4-star reads between
        /// <see cref="StarBandFloor"/> and <see cref="StarBandCeiling"/> of that median.
        ///
        /// THE FLOOR IS ENFORCED ONLY WHERE THE MEDIAN IS ONE. A role with fewer than
        /// <see cref="StarBandMinFives"/> authored 5-stars prints its ratio and is named as
        /// ungraded, because the floor is a claim about where the middle of a role sits and one or
        /// two readings have no middle. The ceiling above keeps running for those roles, because
        /// "smaller than the 5-stars of your role" still means something against one of them.
        ///
        /// EVERY TIER BELOW FIVE. Each has its own pair of edges against the same 5-star median,
        /// because "smaller" is a claim about a tier rather than about the roster: a 3-star graded
        /// against the 4-star band would pass at the bottom of it and say nothing. Four rungs are
        /// graded now that the roster is authored to 94 of 94, and the rule that set the last two
        /// is written out beside <see cref="StarBandTwoFloor"/>.
        ///
        /// AND EACH TIER ALSO HAS TO SIT UNDER ITS OWN ROLE'S TIER ABOVE, which the band against
        /// the 5-star median cannot say on its own. Adjacent bands overlap - x0.45 to x0.60 for
        /// four against three, x0.30 to x0.36 for three against two, x0.18 to x0.20 for two
        /// against one - so a 3-star at x0.58 and a 4-star at x0.47 in the same role both satisfy
        /// their own edges while standing in the wrong order. The tier-above median is the second
        /// reading, and it is a median for the same reason the 5-star one is: a role's 4-stars are
        /// not equal, and the weakest of them is a number about the arena as much as about the
        /// hero. Roles with no authored hero in the tier above have nothing to compare against and
        /// are skipped rather than guessed, and this reading needs no minimum sample, so it runs
        /// in the roles whose band is not enforced too.
        ///
        /// WHAT IT CANNOT SEE, said here rather than discovered later: the arena fields no talent,
        /// so the three a 5-star carries and a 4-star does not are outside this reading and the real
        /// gap is wider than the number. That is why the ceiling is 0.85 rather than 1.00 - a
        /// 4-star reading nine tenths of a 5-star WITHOUT the 5-star's three talents is not a
        /// smaller hero. A clause needing a Taunt enemy, an aspect matchup or an
        /// ally this squad does not have is unmeasured here exactly as it is in the sweep. And a
        /// role whose 5-stars are all unauthored has no median at all, so its 4-stars are listed
        /// and not graded.
        /// </summary>
        private static void CheckStarBandByRole(ContentDatabase live)
        {
            Line("STAR BAND BY ROLE (each authored hero's own output in the sweep arena, five seeds:");
            Line("                   damage dealt + healing paid + shielding granted, credited by source)");

            ContentDatabase arena = BuildHeroSweepArena(live);
            List<HeroDef> fillers = BuildHeroSweepFillers();
            StageDef stage = arena == null ? null : arena.GetStage("sweep_arena");
            if (arena == null || fillers == null || stage == null)
            { Check(false, "star-band arena built"); Line(""); return; }

            List<HeroDef> authored = new List<HeroDef>();
            for (int i = 0; i < live.Heroes.Count; i++)
            {
                HeroDef h = live.Heroes[i];
                if (h.BasicAttack != null && h.BasicAttack.Id.Contains("placeholder")) continue;
                authored.Add(h);
            }
            if (authored.Count == 0) { Check(false, "star-band has authored heroes to measure"); Line(""); return; }

            Dictionary<string, long> output = new Dictionary<string, long>();
            Dictionary<string, long[]> split = new Dictionary<string, long[]>();
            for (int h = 0; h < authored.Count; h++)
            {
                HeroDef def = arena.GetHero(authored[h].Id);
                if (def == null) continue;
                long[] terms = new long[3];
                for (int seed = 1; seed <= HeroSweepSeeds; seed++)
                {
                    List<HeroInstance> team = new List<HeroInstance>();
                    team.Add(MakeInstance(def, 40));
                    for (int f = 0; f < fillers.Count; f++) team.Add(MakeInstance(fillers[f], 40));
                    BattleResult r = RunWith(arena, stage, HeroSweepSeed(def.Id, seed), team);
                    if (r == null) continue;
                    SubjectOutput(r, def.Id, terms);
                }
                output[authored[h].Id] = terms[0] + terms[1] + terms[2];
                split[authored[h].Id] = terms;
            }

            List<string> under = new List<string>();
            List<string> over = new List<string>();
            List<string> tooSmall = new List<string>();
            List<string> tooBig = new List<string>();
            List<string> inBand = new List<string>();
            List<string> tooSmall3 = new List<string>();
            List<string> tooBig3 = new List<string>();
            List<string> inBand3 = new List<string>();
            List<string> tooSmall2 = new List<string>();
            List<string> tooBig2 = new List<string>();
            List<string> inBand2 = new List<string>();
            List<string> tooSmall1 = new List<string>();
            List<string> tooBig1 = new List<string>();
            List<string> inBand1 = new List<string>();
            List<string> overFour = new List<string>();
            List<string> underFour = new List<string>();
            List<string> overThree = new List<string>();
            List<string> underThree = new List<string>();
            List<string> overTwo = new List<string>();
            List<string> underTwo = new List<string>();
            List<string> ungradedRoles = new List<string>();
            List<string> fivesOut = new List<string>();
            List<string> fivesIn = new List<string>();
            List<string> rareOut = new List<string>();
            List<string> rareIn = new List<string>();
            List<string> rarePinned = new List<string>();
            List<string> rareStale = new List<string>();
            int graded = 0;
            Array roles = Enum.GetValues(typeof(HeroRole));
            for (int ri = 0; ri < roles.Length; ri++)
            {
                HeroRole role = (HeroRole)roles.GetValue(ri);
                List<HeroDef> inRole = new List<HeroDef>();
                for (int h = 0; h < authored.Count; h++)
                    if (authored[h].Role == role && output.ContainsKey(authored[h].Id)) inRole.Add(authored[h]);
                if (inRole.Count == 0) continue;

                List<long> fives = new List<long>();
                for (int h = 0; h < inRole.Count; h++)
                    if (inRole[h].Stars >= 5) fives.Add(output[inRole[h].Id]);
                fives.Sort();
                long median = fives.Count == 0 ? -1
                            : fives.Count % 2 == 1 ? fives[fives.Count / 2]
                            : (fives[fives.Count / 2 - 1] + fives[fives.Count / 2]) / 2;

                // A MEDIAN OVER ONE OR TWO HEROES IS NOT A MEDIAN. The ceiling below survives
                // a thin role because "smaller than the 5-stars of your role" still means
                // something against one of them; the BAND does not, because its floor is a claim
                // about where the middle of that role sits, and two readings have no middle.
                // Those roles print their ratio and are named as ungraded rather than silently
                // enforced against a sample that cannot carry the claim.
                bool bandEnforced = fives.Count >= StarBandMinFives;
                if (median > 0 && !bandEnforced) ungradedRoles.Add(role + " (" + fives.Count + ")");

                // THE SECOND READING, and it needs no minimum. The 5-star median is a claim about
                // where the middle of a role sits and wants three samples to mean anything; this
                // one is a claim about ORDER between two adjacent tiers, and one 4-star is still a
                // 4-star a 3-star has to be smaller than. A role with none is skipped. One per
                // rung, so every tier below four is asked the same question about the tier above.
                long fourMedian = TierMedian(inRole, output, 4);
                long threeMedian = TierMedian(inRole, output, 3);
                long twoMedian = TierMedian(inRole, output, 2);

                Line("  " + role + (median < 0
                        ? "  (no authored 5-star, so nothing to grade against)"
                        : "  5-star median " + median + " over " + fives.Count + " hero(es)"
                          + (bandEnforced ? "" : " - BAND NOT ENFORCED, fewer than "
                             + StarBandMinFives)));

                inRole.Sort(delegate (HeroDef a, HeroDef b)
                {
                    if (a.Stars != b.Stars) return b.Stars - a.Stars;
                    return string.CompareOrdinal(a.Id, b.Id);
                });
                for (int h = 0; h < inRole.Count; h++)
                {
                    HeroDef def = inRole[h];
                    long mine = output[def.Id];
                    string ratio = "";
                    // The 5-star against its own role's 5-star median, graded wherever the role
                    // has two or more - see FiveStarFloor.
                    if (median > 0 && def.Stars >= 5)
                    {
                        float fiveShare = mine / (float)median;
                        string reading = def.Id + " x" + fiveShare.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                        ratio = "   x" + fiveShare.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + " of the role's 5-star median";
                        if (fives.Count >= 2)
                        {
                            float floor = FiveStarFloorFor(live, def);
                            bool outside = fiveShare < floor || fiveShare > FiveStarCeiling;
                            // The band grades every 5-star against x0.78 to x1.64 as it always did;
                            // F8's floor for the rarer aspects is its own check below.
                            if (fiveShare < FiveStarFloor || fiveShare > FiveStarCeiling) fivesOut.Add(reading);
                            else fivesIn.Add(reading);
                            if (floor > FiveStarFloor)
                            {
                                string rare = def.Id + " (" + def.Aspect + ") x" + fiveShare.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                                bool pinned = KnownF8Exceptions.ContainsKey(def.Id);
                                if (fiveShare < floor && pinned) rarePinned.Add(rare + " - " + KnownF8Exceptions[def.Id]);
                                else if (fiveShare < floor) rareOut.Add(rare);
                                else if (pinned) rareStale.Add(rare);
                                else rareIn.Add(rare);
                            }
                            ratio += outside ? (fiveShare < floor
                                ? (floor > FiveStarFloor ? "   BELOW ITS ASPECT'S x1.00 FLOOR (F8)" : "   BELOW THE 5-STAR FLOOR")
                                : "   ABOVE THE 5-STAR CEILING") : "";
                        }
                        else ratio += "   (the role's only 5-star)";
                    }
                    if (median > 0 && def.Stars < 5)
                    {
                        float share = mine / (float)median;
                        ratio = "   x" + share.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                                + " of the 5-star median";
                        graded++;
                        if (mine < median) under.Add(def.Id + " x" + share.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
                        else over.Add(def.Id + " " + mine + " against median " + median);

                        // THE BAND, one pair of edges per tier. Four rungs are graded now that the
                        // roster is authored to 94 of 94, and each reads against the same 5-star
                        // median; a tier graded against its neighbour's edges would pass at the
                        // bottom of them and say nothing.
                        if (def.Stars == 4)
                        {
                            string reading = def.Id + " x"
                                + share.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                            if (!bandEnforced) { }
                            else if (share < StarBandFloor) tooSmall.Add(reading);
                            else if (share > StarBandCeiling) tooBig.Add(reading);
                            else inBand.Add(reading);

                            ratio += bandEnforced
                                ? (share < StarBandFloor ? "   BELOW THE BAND"
                                   : share > StarBandCeiling ? "   ABOVE THE BAND" : "   in band")
                                : "   (band not enforced for this role)";
                        }

                        // The tier below, against its own pair of edges.
                        if (def.Stars == 3)
                        {
                            string reading = def.Id + " x"
                                + share.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                            if (!bandEnforced) { }
                            else if (share < StarBandThreeFloor) tooSmall3.Add(reading);
                            else if (share > StarBandThreeCeiling) tooBig3.Add(reading);
                            else inBand3.Add(reading);

                            ratio += bandEnforced
                                ? (share < StarBandThreeFloor ? "   BELOW THE 3-STAR BAND"
                                   : share > StarBandThreeCeiling ? "   ABOVE THE 3-STAR BAND"
                                   : "   in the 3-star band")
                                : "   (band not enforced for this role)";
                        }

                        if (def.Stars == 2)
                        {
                            string reading = def.Id + " x"
                                + share.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                            if (!bandEnforced) { }
                            else if (share < StarBandTwoFloor) tooSmall2.Add(reading);
                            else if (share > StarBandTwoCeiling) tooBig2.Add(reading);
                            else inBand2.Add(reading);

                            ratio += bandEnforced
                                ? (share < StarBandTwoFloor ? "   BELOW THE 2-STAR BAND"
                                   : share > StarBandTwoCeiling ? "   ABOVE THE 2-STAR BAND"
                                   : "   in the 2-star band")
                                : "   (band not enforced for this role)";
                        }

                        if (def.Stars == 1)
                        {
                            string reading = def.Id + " x"
                                + share.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
                            if (!bandEnforced) { }
                            else if (share < StarBandOneFloor) tooSmall1.Add(reading);
                            else if (share > StarBandOneCeiling) tooBig1.Add(reading);
                            else inBand1.Add(reading);

                            ratio += bandEnforced
                                ? (share < StarBandOneFloor ? "   BELOW THE 1-STAR BAND"
                                   : share > StarBandOneCeiling ? "   ABOVE THE 1-STAR BAND"
                                   : "   in the 1-star band")
                                : "   (band not enforced for this role)";
                        }
                    }

                    // The order check, asked of every hero in a role that has an authored hero one
                    // tier up, graded or not. It is a different question from the band and it is
                    // answerable in roles where the band is not.
                    if (def.Stars == 3 && fourMedian > 0)
                    {
                        float fourShare = mine / (float)fourMedian;
                        ratio += "   x" + fourShare.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                                 + " of the 4-star median";
                        if (mine >= fourMedian)
                            overFour.Add(def.Id + " " + mine + " against 4-star median " + fourMedian);
                        else
                            underFour.Add(def.Id + " x"
                                + fourShare.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
                    }
                    if (def.Stars == 2 && threeMedian > 0)
                    {
                        float threeShare = mine / (float)threeMedian;
                        ratio += "   x" + threeShare.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                                 + " of the 3-star median";
                        if (mine >= threeMedian)
                            overThree.Add(def.Id + " " + mine + " against 3-star median " + threeMedian);
                        else
                            underThree.Add(def.Id + " x"
                                + threeShare.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
                    }
                    if (def.Stars == 1 && twoMedian > 0)
                    {
                        float twoShare = mine / (float)twoMedian;
                        ratio += "   x" + twoShare.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                                 + " of the 2-star median";
                        if (mine >= twoMedian)
                            overTwo.Add(def.Id + " " + mine + " against 2-star median " + twoMedian);
                        else
                            underTwo.Add(def.Id + " x"
                                + twoShare.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
                    }
                    long[] terms = split[def.Id];
                    Line("    " + def.Stars + "*  " + def.Id + "  " + mine
                         + "  (dmg " + terms[0] + ", heal " + terms[1] + ", ward " + terms[2] + ")"
                         + ratio);
                }
            }

            if (graded == 0)
            {
                Line("  [note] no authored hero below five stars shares a role with an authored"
                     + " 5-star, so the band has nothing to grade yet.");
                Line("");
                return;
            }
            Check(fivesOut.Count == 0,
                  fivesOut.Count == 0
                    ? "all " + fivesIn.Count + " 5-star(s) in a role with two or more read between x"
                      + FiveStarFloor.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + " and x"
                      + FiveStarCeiling.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                      + " of that role's 5-star median, half a tier step down and a whole one up ("
                      + string.Join(", ", fivesIn.ToArray()) + ")"
                    : fivesOut.Count + " 5-star(s) sit further from their own role's 5-star median than half a tier "
                      + "step down or a whole one up, so the yardstick every lower tier is graded against is "
                      + "not the middle of a tier: " + string.Join(", ", fivesOut.ToArray()));

            Check(rareOut.Count == 0,
                  rareOut.Count == 0
                    ? "all " + rareIn.Count + " 5-star(s) of a seed-recipe aspect in a role with two or more read at or above"
                      + " that role's 5-star median, the x1.00 floor F8 gives the rarer aspects ("
                      + string.Join(", ", rareIn.ToArray()) + ")"
                    : rareOut.Count + " 5-star(s) of a seed-recipe aspect read under their role's 5-star median, the x1.00"
                      + " floor F8 gives the rarer aspects: " + string.Join(", ", rareOut.ToArray()));
            Check(rareStale.Count == 0,
                  rareStale.Count == 0
                    ? (KnownF8Exceptions.Count == 0
                        ? "no F8 exception is pinned: every seed-recipe 5-star is graded against the x1.00 floor"
                        : "every pinned F8 exception is still under the floor, so each pin is still needed ("
                          + string.Join("; ", rarePinned.ToArray()) + ")")
                    : "pinned F8 exception(s) now read at or above their median - remove them from KnownF8Exceptions: "
                      + string.Join(", ", rareStale.ToArray()));

            Check(over.Count == 0,
                  over.Count == 0
                    ? "all " + graded + " authored hero(es) below five stars sit under the 5-star"
                      + " median for their own role (" + string.Join(", ", under.ToArray()) + ")"
                    : over.Count + " hero(es) below five stars match or beat the 5-star median for"
                      + " their own role: " + string.Join("; ", over.ToArray()));

            if (ungradedRoles.Count > 0)
                Line("  [note] the band is NOT enforced for " + string.Join(", ", ungradedRoles.ToArray())
                     + " - fewer than " + StarBandMinFives + " authored 5-stars, so that role's"
                     + " median is a sample rather than a middle. Their 4-stars print a ratio and"
                     + " are not graded on it.");

            int banded = tooSmall.Count + tooBig.Count + inBand.Count;
            if (banded == 0)
            {
                Line("  [note] no authored 4-star sits in a role with " + StarBandMinFives
                     + " or more authored 5-stars, so the band has nothing to enforce yet.");
                Line("");
                return;
            }
            Check(tooSmall.Count == 0 && tooBig.Count == 0,
                  tooSmall.Count == 0 && tooBig.Count == 0
                    ? "all " + banded + " graded 4-star(s) read between x"
                      + StarBandFloor.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + " and x"
                      + StarBandCeiling.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                      + " of their role's 5-star median ("
                      + string.Join(", ", inBand.ToArray()) + ")"
                    : (tooSmall.Count + tooBig.Count) + " graded 4-star(s) fall outside the x"
                      + StarBandFloor.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + " to x"
                      + StarBandCeiling.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                      + " band: "
                      + (tooSmall.Count > 0 ? "below - " + string.Join(", ", tooSmall.ToArray()) : "")
                      + (tooSmall.Count > 0 && tooBig.Count > 0 ? "; " : "")
                      + (tooBig.Count > 0 ? "above - " + string.Join(", ", tooBig.ToArray()) : ""));

            int banded3 = tooSmall3.Count + tooBig3.Count + inBand3.Count;
            if (banded3 > 0)
                Check(tooSmall3.Count == 0 && tooBig3.Count == 0,
                      tooSmall3.Count == 0 && tooBig3.Count == 0
                        ? "all " + banded3 + " graded 3-star(s) read between x"
                          + StarBandThreeFloor.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                          + " and x"
                          + StarBandThreeCeiling.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                          + " of their role's 5-star median ("
                          + string.Join(", ", inBand3.ToArray()) + ")"
                        : (tooSmall3.Count + tooBig3.Count) + " graded 3-star(s) fall outside the x"
                          + StarBandThreeFloor.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                          + " to x"
                          + StarBandThreeCeiling.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                          + " band: "
                          + (tooSmall3.Count > 0 ? "below - " + string.Join(", ", tooSmall3.ToArray()) : "")
                          + (tooSmall3.Count > 0 && tooBig3.Count > 0 ? "; " : "")
                          + (tooBig3.Count > 0 ? "above - " + string.Join(", ", tooBig3.ToArray()) : ""));
            else
                Line("  [note] no authored 3-star sits in a role with " + StarBandMinFives
                     + " or more authored 5-stars, so the 3-star band has nothing to enforce yet.");

            TierBandCheck(2, StarBandTwoFloor, StarBandTwoCeiling, tooSmall2, tooBig2, inBand2);
            TierBandCheck(1, StarBandOneFloor, StarBandOneCeiling, tooSmall1, tooBig1, inBand1);

            TierOrderCheck(3, 4, overFour, underFour);
            TierOrderCheck(2, 3, overThree, underThree);
            TierOrderCheck(1, 2, overTwo, underTwo);
            Line("");
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// THE STAT-STICK BESIDE THE SUBJECT MUST NOT BE STURDIER THAN THE SUBJECT.
        ///
        /// A guard written after the thing it guards against happened. The sweep arena's hitter
        /// aims at `LowestHpEnemy`, so the smallest body on the player line is the one it works
        /// on, and the four fillers exist to stand there and contribute nothing. A filler that is
        /// sturdier than the subject contributes the largest thing in the arena: it hands the
        /// hitter to the hero being measured. Three of the ninety-four - the two flimsiest
        /// Tinkerers and the flimsiest Marksman, all at one star - sat under FillerD's built HP
        /// and died in round one of every seed, reading 0 output whatever their ability numbers
        /// said. See <see cref="BuildHeroSweepFillers"/> for the fix and what it moved.
        ///
        /// Measured through StatMath at the sweep's own level, not off the declared numbers,
        /// because the declared numbers are not the comparison: StarMultiplier is what made a
        /// filler declared at 600 stand above a hero declared at 1122.
        /// </summary>
        private static void CheckSweepFillerIsFlimsiest(ContentDatabase live)
        {
            Line("SWEEP FILLERS (the stat-sticks must be flimsier than every hero they stand beside)");

            List<HeroDef> fillers = BuildHeroSweepFillers();
            if (fillers == null || fillers.Count == 0)
            { Check(false, "sweep fillers built"); Line(""); return; }

            int weakestFiller = int.MaxValue;
            string fillerId = "";
            for (int i = 0; i < fillers.Count; i++)
            {
                int hp = (int)Emberfall.Core.Progression.StatMath.ForHero(fillers[i], HeroSweepLevel).MaxHp;
                if (hp < weakestFiller) { weakestFiller = hp; fillerId = fillers[i].Id; }
            }

            int weakestHero = int.MaxValue;
            string heroId = "";
            for (int i = 0; i < live.Heroes.Count; i++)
            {
                HeroDef def = live.Heroes[i];
                int hp = (int)Emberfall.Core.Progression.StatMath.ForHero(def, HeroSweepLevel).MaxHp;
                if (hp < weakestHero) { weakestHero = hp; heroId = def.Id; }
            }

            Line("  at level " + HeroSweepLevel + ": flimsiest filler " + fillerId + " " + weakestFiller
                 + ", flimsiest hero " + heroId + " " + weakestHero);
            Check(weakestFiller < weakestHero,
                  weakestFiller < weakestHero
                    ? "the arena's flimsiest filler (" + fillerId + ", " + weakestFiller
                      + " HP) is under the roster's flimsiest hero (" + heroId + ", " + weakestHero
                      + " HP), so no subject is ever the hitter's first pick at full health"
                    : "the arena's flimsiest filler (" + fillerId + ", " + weakestFiller
                      + " HP) is not under the roster's flimsiest hero (" + heroId + ", "
                      + weakestHero + " HP), so that hero is the hitter's first pick in its own"
                      + " sweep and the reading is about the arena rather than the kit");
            Line("");
        }

        /// <summary>The middle reading of one tier inside one role, or -1 where that role has
        /// nobody in it. Shared by the band's four rungs so the four cannot drift apart.</summary>
        private static long TierMedian(List<HeroDef> inRole, Dictionary<string, long> output, int stars)
        {
            List<long> tier = new List<long>();
            for (int h = 0; h < inRole.Count; h++)
                if (inRole[h].Stars == stars) tier.Add(output[inRole[h].Id]);
            if (tier.Count == 0) return -1;
            tier.Sort();
            return tier.Count % 2 == 1 ? tier[tier.Count / 2]
                 : (tier[tier.Count / 2 - 1] + tier[tier.Count / 2]) / 2;
        }

        /// <summary>One tier against its own pair of edges, worded the way the 4-star rung above
        /// words it.</summary>
        private static void TierBandCheck(int stars, float floor, float ceiling,
                                          List<string> tooSmall, List<string> tooBig, List<string> inBand)
        {
            int banded = tooSmall.Count + tooBig.Count + inBand.Count;
            string tier = stars + "-star";
            if (banded == 0)
            {
                Line("  [note] no authored " + tier + " sits in a role with " + StarBandMinFives
                     + " or more authored 5-stars, so the " + tier
                     + " band has nothing to enforce yet.");
                return;
            }
            Check(tooSmall.Count == 0 && tooBig.Count == 0,
                  tooSmall.Count == 0 && tooBig.Count == 0
                    ? "all " + banded + " graded " + tier + "(s) read between x"
                      + floor.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + " and x"
                      + ceiling.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
                      + " of their role's 5-star median ("
                      + string.Join(", ", inBand.ToArray()) + ")"
                    : (tooSmall.Count + tooBig.Count) + " graded " + tier + "(s) fall outside the x"
                      + floor.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + " to x"
                      + ceiling.ToString("0.00", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + " band: "
                      + (tooSmall.Count > 0 ? "below - " + string.Join(", ", tooSmall.ToArray()) : "")
                      + (tooSmall.Count > 0 && tooBig.Count > 0 ? "; " : "")
                      + (tooBig.Count > 0 ? "above - " + string.Join(", ", tooBig.ToArray()) : ""));
        }

        /// <summary>One tier against the median of the tier directly above it, in every role that
        /// has one. Needs no minimum sample: this is a claim about ORDER, not about a middle.
        /// </summary>
        private static void TierOrderCheck(int stars, int above, List<string> over, List<string> under)
        {
            int ordered = over.Count + under.Count;
            if (ordered == 0) return;
            Check(over.Count == 0,
                  over.Count == 0
                    ? "all " + ordered + " " + stars + "-star(s) in a role with an authored "
                      + above + "-star sit under that role's " + above + "-star median ("
                      + string.Join(", ", under.ToArray()) + ")"
                    : over.Count + " " + stars + "-star(s) match or beat the " + above
                      + "-star median for their own role, so the two tiers stand in the wrong"
                      + " order: " + string.Join("; ", over.ToArray()));
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// What one unit put into a battle: damage it dealt, healing it paid out and shielding it
        /// granted, by SOURCE id. A DoT tick credits whoever applied it, because that is the id the
        /// tick carries - which is the right answer for a kit whose output IS its burn.
        /// </summary>
        private static void SubjectOutput(BattleResult r, string defId, long[] terms)
        {
            int id = -1;
            for (int i = 0; i < r.Units.Count; i++)
                if (r.Units[i].IsPlayer && r.Units[i].DefId == defId) { id = r.Units[i].Id; break; }
            if (id < 0) return;
            for (int i = 0; i < r.Events.Count; i++)
            {
                BattleEvent e = r.Events[i];
                if (e.SourceId != id) continue;
                if (e.Type == BattleEventType.Damage) terms[0] += e.Value;
                else if (e.Type == BattleEventType.Heal) terms[1] += e.Value;
                else if (e.Type == BattleEventType.ShieldGain) terms[2] += e.Value;
            }
        }

        // ---- which of a new kit's own rules the sweep actually reached -------------------------

        /// <summary>
        /// A REPORT, NOT A GUARD, and deliberately so.
        ///
        /// The sweep proves a kit's line is its own and that it has not moved. It cannot say
        /// whether the kit DID anything: a passive wired to a trigger nothing in the arena raises
        /// fingerprints exactly like one that fires every round, because both are the same absence
        /// of events. Four kits authored in one pass on six triggers this roster had never used is
        /// the case where that distinction matters most, so it is printed.
        ///
        /// No Check, because "never reached" is not a defect on its own - OnAllyDefeated needs an
        /// ally to die, and an arena where that never happens is a fact about the arena. An
        /// assertion here would be a guess dressed as a guarantee, and a line that reads "0" is
        /// evidence a human can act on without one.
        ///
        /// A COUNT HERE IS THE TRIGGER ARRIVING, NOT THE CLAUSE HAPPENING. TriggerPassive emits its
        /// event and then evaluates each effect's own condition, so a passive whose every effect is
        /// gated on something false still counts. Nysera's 80 is exactly that: OnTargeted reaches
        /// her on every blow aimed at her, and each of her three effects then asks whether the
        /// enemy aiming carries her Mark.
        ///
        /// Talents are excluded: none is selected here, so all three would read zero for every
        /// hero and say nothing about any of them.
        /// </summary>
        private static void CheckAuthoredKitExercise(ContentDatabase live)
        {
            Line("AUTHORED-KIT EXERCISE (how often each kit's own rules were REACHED in the sweep");
            Line("                       arena, five seeds - a trigger reaching a passive is not the");
            Line("                       same as its effects passing their conditions)");

            string[] subjects = { "talren_the_wayfinder", "vaelis_the_unfixed_blade",
                                  "nysera_the_missing_arrow", "rovan_the_adaptive_commander",
                                  "kiva_the_forsaken_oath", "sylven_the_unfading_blade",
                                  "merek_the_sequence_thief", "oris_the_unwritten_path",
                                  "zella_the_slow_argument", "dahlia_the_wayward_hymn",
                                  // The first 4-star batch, here for the reason the ten above
                                  // are: each of the five hangs a described clause on a trigger
                                  // and a zero would mean the arena never raised it.
                                  "branik_the_siege_mason", "demos_the_debt_collector",
                                  "kest_the_bridgebreaker", "orelle_the_fever_warden",
                                  "tavia_the_signal_huntress",
                                  // The second 4-star batch, same reason.
                                  "celen_the_open_hand", "mika_the_salvage_pilot",
                                  "rhys_the_fence_walker", "siofra_the_turncoat_oracle",
                                  "vanno_the_detour_king",
                                  // The third 4-star batch, same reason.
                                  "corin_the_patience_engineer", "halwen_the_reserve_keeper",
                                  "jory_the_old_gate", "maera_the_harbor_eye",
                                  "maren_the_long_sleeper", "eira_the_rolling_barricade",
                                  // The fourth, both halves. With these the 4-star tier is
                                  // complete at twenty-six.
                                  "finn_the_bellmaker", "niko_the_uninvited_guest",
                                  "sable_the_queue_cutter", "tamsin_the_loose_thread",
                                  "iolan_the_redacted_judge", "ruma_the_false_trail",
                                  "toren_the_closed_door", "leon_the_numbered_arrow",
                                  "lio_the_clear_diagnosis", "nadine_the_border_clerk",
                                  // The first 3-star batch. Same reason again, and one of them is
                                  // the first kit in the roster to listen on a miss, which is a
                                  // window this arena had never opened.
                                  "adric_the_blank_map", "aster_the_glassblower",
                                  "quin_the_sealed_dispatch", "selka_the_unheard_appeal",
                                  "bren_the_extra_measure", "cal_the_alternate_angle",
                                  // The second 3-star batch, same reason.
                                  "linnet_the_misdialed", "oswin_the_refit_surgeon",
                                  "brio_the_patient_record", "elin_the_second_stitch",
                                  "marn_the_holding_wall", "rusk_the_patchmaker",
                                  // The third 3-star batch. With these the 3-star tier is
                                  // complete at eighteen.
                                  "avera_the_interrupter", "lydia_the_unscheduled",
                                  "roan_the_side_door", "tomas_the_door_jammer",
                                  "petra_the_unsent_letter", "clara_the_named_remedy",
                                  // The 2-star tier, all twelve. Each is a basic and exactly one
                                  // passive, so a zero here is the whole of that hero's rule
                                  // never being reached rather than one clause of several. The
                                  // 1-stars are deliberately absent: a 1-star is a basic and
                                  // nothing else, so it has no rule for this report to count.
                                  "arno_the_unfinished_mason", "barr_the_splintered_door",
                                  "piri_the_match_seller", "dett_the_untied_knot",
                                  "hod_the_redirected_load", "yara_the_spare_compass",
                                  "garron_the_stout_frame", "nara_the_road_nurse",
                                  "wynn_the_hand_me_down", "jax_the_clockwork_rat",
                                  "kora_the_interrupted_queue", "sena_the_empty_chair" };

            ContentDatabase arena = BuildHeroSweepArena(live);
            List<HeroDef> fillers = BuildHeroSweepFillers();
            StageDef stage = arena == null ? null : arena.GetStage("sweep_arena");
            if (arena == null || fillers == null || stage == null)
            { Check(false, "authored-kit exercise arena built"); Line(""); return; }

            for (int h = 0; h < subjects.Length; h++)
            {
                HeroDef def = arena.GetHero(subjects[h]);
                if (def == null) { Line("  [note] " + subjects[h] + " not in content"); continue; }

                Dictionary<string, int> fires = new Dictionary<string, int>();
                for (int p = 0; p < def.Passives.Count; p++)
                    if (def.Passives[p].Trigger != PassiveTrigger.None)
                        fires[def.Passives[p].Id] = 0;
                int basics = 0, actives = 0;

                for (int seed = 1; seed <= HeroSweepSeeds; seed++)
                {
                    List<HeroInstance> team = new List<HeroInstance>();
                    team.Add(MakeInstance(def, 40));
                    for (int f = 0; f < fillers.Count; f++) team.Add(MakeInstance(fillers[f], 40));
                    BattleResult r = RunWith(arena, stage, HeroSweepSeed(def.Id, seed), team);
                    if (r == null) continue;

                    for (int i = 0; i < r.Events.Count; i++)
                    {
                        BattleEvent e = r.Events[i];
                        if (e.Type == BattleEventType.PassiveTrigger && e.AbilityId != null
                            && fires.ContainsKey(e.AbilityId)) fires[e.AbilityId]++;
                        else if (e.Type == BattleEventType.BasicAttack
                                 && def.BasicAttack != null && e.AbilityId == def.BasicAttack.Id) basics++;
                        else if (e.Type == BattleEventType.AbilityCast
                                 && def.Active != null && e.AbilityId == def.Active.Id) actives++;
                    }
                }

                StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
                sb.Append("  [note] ").Append(def.Name).Append(": ").Append(basics)
                  .Append(" basic(s) cast, ").Append(actives).Append(" active(s) cast");
                foreach (KeyValuePair<string, int> kv in fires)
                    sb.Append(" | ").Append(kv.Key).Append(' ').Append(kv.Value);
                Line(sb.ToString());
            }
            Line("");
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// EVERY AUTHORED ACTIVE IS SEEN BEING SPENT, and the sweep arena cannot show one of them.
        ///
        /// The exercise above reports what a kit's own rules did, and Roan's line read "10 basic(s)
        /// cast, 0 active(s) cast" - the only hero in the roster with an authored active whose
        /// active the sweep never raises. It is not his kit. Through the Service Door costs the
        /// same 100 Energy every 3-star active costs, and Selka, who is the same role at the same
        /// stars with the same stat line, casts hers. Roan dies in round 3 of all five seeds, a
        /// turn before the bar he has already filled would be spent: he reaches 100 Energy in every
        /// run and never gets the turn to use it. A sweep line that says "this ability was never
        /// measured" is a hole, not a reading, so this check names the hole and then closes it
        /// somewhere the ability can be seen.
        ///
        /// TWO HALVES. The first walks every authored active over the sweep's own arena and seeds
        /// and asserts that the set of never-cast ones is EXACTLY Roan - so a second hero going
        /// quiet is a failure rather than a silence, and Roan going quiet somewhere else is too.
        /// The second runs Roan alone against s_triple, one of the dark arenas, chosen for the two
        /// things his active needs and the sweep will not give him: a body that cannot kill him
        /// before round 4, so he lives to spend the bar, and a body that holds long buffs, so the
        /// Dispel half has something to take. Nothing in the sweep arena moves, so no other check
        /// loses anything.
        /// </summary>
        private static void CheckEveryAuthoredActiveIsCast(ContentDatabase live)
        {
            Line("AUTHORED ACTIVES (every hero that has one has to be seen spending it)");

            ContentDatabase arena = BuildHeroSweepArena(live);
            List<HeroDef> fillers = BuildHeroSweepFillers();
            StageDef stage = arena == null ? null : arena.GetStage("sweep_arena");
            if (arena == null || fillers == null || stage == null)
            { Check(false, "authored-active sweep arena built"); Line(""); return; }

            List<string> silent = new List<string>();
            int withActive = 0;
            for (int h = 0; h < arena.Heroes.Count; h++)
            {
                HeroDef def = arena.Heroes[h];
                if (def.Active == null) continue;
                withActive++;
                int casts = 0;
                for (int seed = 1; seed <= HeroSweepSeeds; seed++)
                {
                    List<HeroInstance> team = new List<HeroInstance>();
                    team.Add(MakeInstance(def, HeroSweepLevel));
                    for (int f = 0; f < fillers.Count; f++) team.Add(MakeInstance(fillers[f], HeroSweepLevel));
                    BattleResult r = RunWith(arena, stage, HeroSweepSeed(def.Id, seed), team);
                    if (r == null) continue;
                    for (int i = 0; i < r.Events.Count; i++)
                        if (r.Events[i].Type == BattleEventType.AbilityCast
                            && r.Events[i].AbilityId == def.Active.Id) casts++;
                }
                if (casts == 0) silent.Add(def.Id);
            }

            Check(silent.Count == 1 && silent[0] == "roan_the_side_door",
                  "of the " + withActive + " authored active(s), exactly one is never cast in the sweep"
                  + " arena and it is Roan's (never cast: "
                  + (silent.Count == 0 ? "none" : string.Join(", ", silent.ToArray()))
                  + ") - he reaches 100 Energy in all five seeds and dies in round 3, a turn before"
                  + " the bar would be spent");

            ContentDatabase dark = DarkArena(false);
            StageDef triple = dark == null ? null : dark.GetStage("s_triple");
            HeroDef roan = dark == null ? null : dark.GetHero("roan_the_side_door");
            if (dark == null || triple == null || roan == null || roan.Active == null)
            { Check(false, "Roan's own arena built"); Line(""); return; }

            List<HeroInstance> line = new List<HeroInstance>();
            line.Add(MakeInstance(roan, HeroSweepLevel));
            BattleResult run = RunWith(dark, triple, 1, line);
            if (run == null) { Check(false, "Roan's own arena resolved"); Line(""); return; }

            int roanId = -1;
            for (int u = 0; u < run.Units.Count; u++)
                if (run.Units[u].DefId == roan.Id) roanId = run.Units[u].Id;

            int casts2 = 0, activeBlow = 0, basicBlow = 0, buffsTaken = 0;
            bool inActive = false, inBasic = false;
            for (int i = 0; i < run.Events.Count; i++)
            {
                BattleEvent e = run.Events[i];
                if (e.Type == BattleEventType.AbilityCast || e.Type == BattleEventType.BasicAttack)
                {
                    inActive = e.SourceId == roanId && e.Type == BattleEventType.AbilityCast
                               && e.AbilityId == roan.Active.Id;
                    inBasic = e.SourceId == roanId && e.Type == BattleEventType.BasicAttack;
                    if (inActive) casts2++;
                    continue;
                }
                if (e.SourceId != roanId) continue;
                if (e.Type == BattleEventType.Damage)
                {
                    if (inActive && e.Value > activeBlow) activeBlow = e.Value;
                    if (inBasic && e.Value > basicBlow) basicBlow = e.Value;
                }
                if (inActive && e.Type == BattleEventType.StatusExpired
                    && e.Status != StatusKind.Mark && e.Status != StatusKind.MarkAlt
                    && !StatusInfo.IsHarmful(e.Status)) buffsTaken++;
            }

            Check(casts2 > 0, "Roan casts Through the Service Door in an arena that lets him live to"
                  + " round 4 (" + casts2 + " cast(s) against 0 across all five sweep seeds)");
            Check(activeBlow > basicBlow && basicBlow > 0,
                  "and its blow lands on the Off Balance body: " + activeBlow + " against Unexpected"
                  + " Entry's " + basicBlow + ", which is the 360% against the 256% - so"
                  + " EnemyWithMyMark resolved rather than the cast fizzling");
            Check(buffsTaken > 0,
                  "and it takes a buff off that body (" + buffsTaken + " stripped) - the Dispel half"
                  + " of the ability, which needs an enemy holding a buff longer than its own turn");
            Line("");
        }

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------- ability numbers

        /// <summary>The level every ability-number proof is taken at. The same 40 the hero sweep
        /// and the star-band grader use, so a number quoted here can be read beside one of
        /// theirs.</summary>
        private const int AbilityNumberLevel = 40;

        /// <summary>
        /// THE CARD'S NUMBER IS THE FIGHT'S NUMBER, proved for every authored basic, active and
        /// passive in the roster rather than asserted.
        ///
        /// Docs/DESIGN-DAMAGE-DISPLAY.md settled that a hero page should lead with a FLAT value at
        /// the hero's own stats and keep the authored percentage beneath it, because a percentage
        /// is priced against its owner's Attack and the star tier has already scaled that Attack
        /// down - which is why a 1-star basic reads 437% against a 5-star's 110%. AbilityMath is
        /// the arithmetic that undoes it. The one thing that can go wrong with a second
        /// implementation of a damage formula is that it drifts away from the first, silently, the
        /// next time somebody adds a term to ApplyDamageEffect. This check is what makes that
        /// noisy.
        ///
        /// HOW IT PROVES IT. For every DAMAGE, HEAL and SHIELD effect on every authored basic,
        /// active and passive - the three kinds the hero page's ability row shows - it builds a
        /// one-effect probe ability, hangs it off the hero's own stat line in the basic slot, and
        /// fires it into an arena chosen so that every term AbilityMath deliberately does not
        /// model is an exact identity:
        ///
        ///   - the dummy's Defence is zero, so the mitigation ratio is exactly 0;
        ///   - the dummy wears the caster's own aspect, so AspectChart.Multiplier is exactly 1
        ///     (an aspect never beats itself);
        ///   - damageVariance is 0, and DeterministicRng.Spread(0) returns exactly 0;
        ///   - the hero's Crit Chance is 0, and DeterministicRng.Chance(0) returns false without
        ///     drawing, so no blow can crit;
        ///   - the hero's passives are cleared, so nothing buffs the Attack the card quoted.
        ///
        /// WHAT THE PROBE STRIPS, AND WHY THAT IS NOT CHEATING. Exactly two things, and both are
        /// things the card explicitly does not claim: the clauses that decide WHETHER an effect
        /// resolves (a conditional blow is worth its number when it lands, and the card says so),
        /// and the target-health terms, which no card outside a fight can know. Power, Flat, Hits,
        /// SelfHpRatio, the Attack cap and the per-stack scaler are left exactly as authored,
        /// because those are what the number is made OF. An effect whose amount is a runtime
        /// quantity outright - the ScaleBy family - has no flat number on the card either, so it
        /// is counted and named here instead of being measured.
        ///
        /// The probe carries the real ability's KIND even though it fires from the basic slot,
        /// because Skill Damage is gated on it: a card that prints an active is proved against an
        /// active.
        /// </summary>
        private static void CheckAbilityNumbersMatchTheSimulation(ContentDatabase live)
        {
            Line("ABILITY NUMBERS (what a hero page prints against what the simulation deals)");

            ContentDatabase arena = BuildAbilityNumberArena();
            List<HeroDef> patients = BuildAbilityNumberPatient();
            StageDef stage = arena == null ? null : arena.GetStage("number_arena");
            EnemyDef dummy = arena == null ? null : arena.GetEnemy("number_dummy");
            if (arena == null || patients == null || patients.Count != 1 || stage == null || dummy == null)
            { Check(false, "ability-number arena built"); Line(""); return; }

            // Variance off. It is read straight off the config at the one place it is applied, so
            // zeroing it here is the whole of turning it off.
            arena.Balance.DamageVariance = 0f;
            arena.Balance.SpeedVariance = 0f;
            HeroDef patient = patients[0];

            int proved = 0, abilities = 0, statLinesChecked = 0;
            List<string> wrong = new List<string>();
            List<string> derived = new List<string>();
            List<string> partial = new List<string>();
            List<string> flatless = new List<string>();
            int conditional = 0, area = 0;

            for (int h = 0; h < arena.Heroes.Count; h++)
            {
                HeroDef def = arena.Heroes[h];

                // The probe hero is this hero's STAT LINE and nothing else of its kit: no
                // passives to buff the Attack the card quoted, no crit, no Energy to spend. The
                // passives are kept in hand first, because their own effects are measured below.
                List<PassiveDef> passives = new List<PassiveDef>(def.Passives);
                def.Passives.Clear();
                def.CritChance = 0f;
                def.StartEnergy = 0;
                AbilityDef realBasic = def.BasicAttack;
                AbilityDef realActive = def.Active;
                def.Active = null;

                // The row on the hero page carries the passives too, and they print through the
                // same formula, so they are proved through the same probe. A passive reads as an
                // ability with a trigger taken off it - AbilityMath.AsAbility is the wrapper both
                // this and the screen use - and its KIND answers the one question the damage
                // pipeline asks: Skill Damage reaches a passive-sourced blow, never a basic.
                List<AbilityDef> slots = new List<AbilityDef>();
                slots.Add(realBasic);
                slots.Add(realActive);
                for (int p = 0; p < passives.Count; p++) slots.Add(AbilityMath.AsAbility(passives[p]));

                // An aspect never beats itself, so a dummy wearing the caster's own aspect makes
                // AspectChart.Multiplier exactly 1. There is no universally neutral aspect to
                // stand in for it - each of the six beats one and is beaten by one.
                dummy.Aspect = def.Aspect;

                UnitStats stats = StatMath.ForHero(def, AbilityNumberLevel);

                for (int slot = 0; slot < slots.Count; slot++)
                {
                    AbilityDef ability = slots[slot];
                    if (ability == null) continue;
                    abilities++;

                    List<AbilityValue> values = AbilityMath.Values(ability, stats, arena.Balance);
                    for (int vi = 0; vi < values.Count; vi++)
                    {
                        AbilityValue v = values[vi];
                        // Keyed by the ability's ID rather than its NAME, because the two are not
                        // the same kind of thing: Maera's active and one of her passives are both
                        // called Keep the Beacon, and a pinned list keyed on names would have one
                        // line standing for two different effects.
                        string who = ability.Id + " effect " + v.EffectIndex;
                        if (v.Conditional) conditional++;
                        if (v.Area) area++;
                        if (v.Partial) partial.Add(who);
                        if (v.Derived) { derived.Add(who); continue; }
                        if (!v.HasFlat) flatless.Add(who);

                        def.BasicAttack = ProbeAbility(ability, ability.Effects[v.EffectIndex], v);
                        float sawAttack;
                        int dealt = FireAbilityNumberProbe(arena, stage, def, patient, v.Kind, out sawAttack);
                        if (dealt == int.MinValue)
                        { wrong.Add(who + ": the probe battle did not resolve"); continue; }

                        // The stat line the simulation built has to be the one the card quoted,
                        // or the comparison below is two different heroes agreeing by accident.
                        if (sawAttack != stats.Attack)
                        { wrong.Add(who + ": card Attack " + stats.Attack + ", simulation " + sawAttack); continue; }
                        statLinesChecked++;

                        if (dealt != v.Amount)
                            wrong.Add(who + ": card " + v.Amount + ", simulation " + dealt);
                        else proved++;
                    }
                }

                def.BasicAttack = realBasic;
                def.Active = realActive;
                for (int p = 0; p < passives.Count; p++) def.Passives.Add(passives[p]);
            }

            Line("  [note] " + arena.Heroes.Count + " heroes, " + abilities + " authored basic(s),"
                 + " active(s) and passive(s), " + (proved + wrong.Count + derived.Count)
                 + " damage/heal/shield effect(s) between them");
            Line("  [note] of those, " + conditional + " carry a clause that gates them and " + area
                 + " land on more than one body - both are stated on the card and neither changes"
                 + " what one application is worth");

            Check(wrong.Count == 0,
                  wrong.Count == 0
                    ? "every one of the " + proved + " printable effect(s) on all " + arena.Heroes.Count
                      + " heroes matches what CombatSimulation deals to a zero-Defence body with crit"
                      + " and variance off (" + statLinesChecked + " stat lines agreed too)"
                    : wrong.Count + " printed number(s) do NOT match the simulation: "
                      + string.Join("; ", wrong.ToArray()));

            // THE THREE ESCAPE HATCHES ARE PINNED BY NAME rather than merely counted. Each is an
            // effect whose card cannot quote the whole truth, and each is a card a player would
            // otherwise trust the same way they trust the other three hundred. A new one has to
            // arrive as a failing check and a deliberate edit here rather than as a silence.
            //
            // The first: the ScaleBy family, whose Power multiplies a runtime amount instead of
            // Attack. There is no flat figure outside a fight, so the card prints the workings.
            string[] expectedDerived =
            {
                "rovan_deflected_conversion effect 0",
                "brenna_deep_resolve effect 0",
                "merek_interjecting_clause effect 2",
                "cyrra_bury_the_burden effect 5",
                "aminah_emergency_release effect 1",
                "aminah_release_damage effect 0"
            };
            Check(SameSet(derived, expectedDerived),
                  "exactly " + expectedDerived.Length + " effect(s) scale off a runtime amount rather"
                  + " than Attack and so print no flat number, and they are the expected ones (found: "
                  + (derived.Count == 0 ? "none" : string.Join(", ", derived.ToArray())) + ")");

            // The second: terms that ADD to the Attack-scaled amount from somewhere the card
            // cannot see. The figure is real and is a floor, and the card says which way it
            // moves rather than pretending the floor is the ceiling.
            string[] expectedPartial =
            {
                "hale_closing_radius effect 1",
                "kiva_sever_the_return effect 2",
                "marrow_promise_heal effect 0",
                // ENGINE-GAPS pass 2: the same Promise heal, on a Promise Marrow holds himself.
                "marrow_promise_heal_self effect 0",
                "demos_arrears_tick effect 0",
                "orelle_hard_remedy effect 2",
                "orelle_triage_after_loss effect 0",
                "nereia_change_of_course effect 1",
                "isara_what_survives effect 1",
                "halwen_rationed_strength effect 1",
                "jory_holdfast_thrust effect 1",
                "maera_keep_the_beacon_relief effect 0",
                "brio_evidence_kept effect 1",
                "marn_relief_after_loss effect 0",
                "garron_set_iron effect 0",
                "hal_cache_shovel effect 1",
                "aeron_press_forward effect 2",
                "tomas_keep_moving effect 0",
                "toren_unasked_question effect 0",
                "arden_find_the_measure effect 0",
                "arden_define_the_weak_point effect 2",
                "arden_define_the_weak_point effect 3",
                "arden_exact_calculation effect 0",
                "arden_exact_calculation effect 1",
                "arden_everything_has_a_limit_payout effect 0",
                "caldus_defined_duty effect 0",
                "leon_close_the_case effect 0",
                "lio_exact_treatment effect 0"
            };
            Check(SameSet(partial, expectedPartial),
                  "exactly " + expectedPartial.Length + " effect(s) add a term the card cannot see -"
                  + " a share of the target's health, or a counter the fight has not built yet - and"
                  + " print their caster-only part as a floor rather than as the whole (found "
                  + partial.Count + ": "
                  + (partial.Count == 0 ? "none" : string.Join(", ", partial.ToArray())) + ")");

            // The third, and a SUBSET of the second - which is why the two are counted apart.
            // Most of the list above still prints a figure, because its caster-side term is real
            // and the target share is extra on top of it. These carry no caster-side term at all -
            // no Power, no Flat, no Max HP share - so their whole payout is a fraction of somebody
            // else's health and there is no number for a card to lead with. It prints the clause
            // instead.
            string[] expectedFlatless =
            {
                "hale_closing_radius effect 1",
                "kiva_sever_the_return effect 2",
                "demos_arrears_tick effect 0",
                "orelle_hard_remedy effect 2",
                "orelle_triage_after_loss effect 0",
                "halwen_rationed_strength effect 1",
                "jory_holdfast_thrust effect 1",
                "maera_keep_the_beacon_relief effect 0",
                "brio_evidence_kept effect 1",
                "marn_relief_after_loss effect 0",
                "garron_set_iron effect 0",
                "hal_cache_shovel effect 1",
                "tomas_keep_moving effect 0",
                "toren_unasked_question effect 0",
                "arden_find_the_measure effect 0",
                "arden_define_the_weak_point effect 2",
                "arden_define_the_weak_point effect 3",
                "arden_exact_calculation effect 0",
                "arden_exact_calculation effect 1",
                "arden_everything_has_a_limit_payout effect 0",
                "lio_exact_treatment effect 0"
            };
            Check(SameSet(flatless, expectedFlatless),
                  "exactly " + expectedFlatless.Length + " effect(s) carry no caster-side term at all"
                  + " and are priced entirely off the target's health, so the card prints their clause"
                  + " rather than a number, and they are the expected ones (found " + flatless.Count
                  + ": " + (flatless.Count == 0 ? "none" : string.Join(", ", flatless.ToArray())) + ")");

            // THE WORKED EXAMPLE THIS WHOLE PASS EXISTS FOR, printed rather than described. The two
            // heroes are the pair Docs/DESIGN-DAMAGE-DISPLAY.md opens with: the cheapest 1-star in
            // the roster and a 5-star, whose authored percentages put them in the wrong order and
            // whose flat values put them in the right one.
            ReportAbilityNumbers(arena, "iri_the_kept_token");
            ReportAbilityNumbers(arena, "kael_the_unyielding");

            Line("");
        }

        /// <summary>One hero's ability numbers as the hero page would print them, at the level the
        /// proof above is taken at. A report line, not a check.</summary>
        private static void ReportAbilityNumbers(ContentDatabase arena, string heroId)
        {
            HeroDef def = arena.GetHero(heroId);
            if (def == null) { Line("  [note] " + heroId + " is not in content"); return; }
            UnitStats stats = StatMath.ForHero(def, AbilityNumberLevel);
            Line("  [note] " + def.Name + ", " + def.Stars + "-star, level " + AbilityNumberLevel
                 + ", Attack " + stats.Attack + ":");
            for (int slot = 0; slot < 2; slot++)
            {
                AbilityDef ability = slot == 0 ? def.BasicAttack : def.Active;
                if (ability == null) continue;
                List<AbilityValue> values = AbilityMath.Values(ability, stats, arena.Balance);
                for (int i = 0; i < values.Count; i++)
                {
                    AbilityValue v = values[i];
                    Line("           " + (slot == 0 ? "basic  " : "active ") + ability.Name
                         + " - " + (v.Derived || !v.HasFlat ? "no flat value" : v.Amount + " " + v.Kind)
                         + " (" + v.Percent.ToString("0.##") + "% of Attack)");
                }
            }
        }

        private static bool SameSet(List<string> found, string[] expected)
        {
            if (found.Count != expected.Length) return false;
            for (int i = 0; i < expected.Length; i++)
            {
                bool hit = false;
                for (int j = 0; j < found.Count; j++) if (found[j] == expected[i]) { hit = true; break; }
                if (!hit) return false;
            }
            return true;
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// One authored effect, alone in an ability, with the two things the card does not claim
        /// taken off it and everything the number is made of left alone.
        /// </summary>
        private static AbilityDef ProbeAbility(AbilityDef source, EffectDef authored, AbilityValue value)
        {
            EffectDef probe = authored.Clone();

            // The clauses that decide WHETHER it resolves. The card prints what the effect is
            // worth when it lands and marks it as conditional; proving the amount is a separate
            // question from proving the gate, and the gate has its own checks.
            probe.Condition = EffectCondition.None;
            probe.Condition2 = EffectCondition.None;
            probe.Condition3 = EffectCondition.None;

            // The target-health terms, which are the only part of the sum that belongs to
            // somebody the card has never met.
            probe.TargetMaxHpRatio = 0f;
            probe.TargetCurrentHpRatio = 0f;
            probe.TargetMissingHpRatio = 0f;

            // A target this arena can always answer. Half the roster's damage aims at a mark, a
            // last attacker or an enemy holding a status, none of which exists on turn one - an
            // effect that resolved to nobody would prove nothing at all.
            probe.Target = value.Kind == AbilityValueKind.Damage
                         ? TargetMode.FrontEnemy : TargetMode.LowestHpAlly;

            AbilityDef ability = new AbilityDef();
            ability.Id = "probe_" + source.Id;
            ability.Name = source.Name;
            // The KIND travels with it even though the probe fires from the basic slot: Skill
            // Damage is gated on it, so a card that prints an active is proved against an active.
            ability.Kind = source.Kind;
            ability.EnergyCost = 0;
            ability.Vfx = source.Vfx;
            ability.Effects.Add(probe);
            return ability;
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Fires the probe once and returns what the hero's FIRST action was worth, summed over
        /// its hits. int.MinValue when the battle did not resolve or the hero was not in it.
        /// </summary>
        private static int FireAbilityNumberProbe(ContentDatabase arena, StageDef stage, HeroDef def,
                                                  HeroDef patient, AbilityValueKind kind, out float attack)
        {
            attack = 0f;
            List<HeroInstance> team = new List<HeroInstance>();
            team.Add(MakeInstance(def, AbilityNumberLevel));
            team.Add(MakeInstance(patient, 1));
            BattleResult r = RunWith(arena, stage, 1, team);
            if (r == null) return int.MinValue;

            int heroId = -1;
            for (int u = 0; u < r.Units.Count; u++)
                if (r.Units[u].DefId == def.Id) { heroId = r.Units[u].Id; attack = r.Units[u].Attack; }
            if (heroId < 0) return int.MinValue;

            BattleEventType want = kind == AbilityValueKind.Damage ? BattleEventType.Damage
                                 : kind == AbilityValueKind.Heal ? BattleEventType.Heal
                                 : BattleEventType.ShieldGain;

            // Everything between the hero's first action and the next action by anybody. The
            // patient wounds itself at BattleStart, which is before any action event, so that blow
            // cannot be counted; the dummy's own turn closes the window.
            int total = 0;
            bool inside = false;
            for (int i = 0; i < r.Events.Count; i++)
            {
                BattleEvent e = r.Events[i];
                if (e.Type == BattleEventType.BasicAttack || e.Type == BattleEventType.AbilityCast)
                {
                    if (inside) break;
                    inside = e.SourceId == heroId;
                    continue;
                }
                if (!inside) continue;
                if (e.Type == want && e.SourceId == heroId) total += e.Value;
            }
            return total;
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// The live roster against one body that reduces nothing.
        ///
        /// Defence zero is the point: the mitigation ratio is defense / (defense + constant + per
        /// level * level), which is exactly 0 when Defence is, so the Defence step of
        /// ApplyDamageEffect becomes a multiply by one and the card's caster-only number is the
        /// whole of what lands. Block, Damage Reduction and Armor Break are zero for the same
        /// reason. Its HP is large enough that nothing in the roster can kill it, so a multi-hit
        /// effect is never cut short by its own first blow.
        /// </summary>
        private static ContentDatabase BuildAbilityNumberArena()
        {
            string dir = FindContentDirForSweep();
            if (dir == null) return null;
            string heroes, balance;
            try
            {
                heroes = File.ReadAllText(Path.Combine(dir, "heroes.json"));
                balance = File.ReadAllText(Path.Combine(dir, "balance.json"));
            }
            catch (Exception) { return null; }

            string enemies = @"{
  ""enemies"": [
    { ""id"": ""number_dummy"", ""name"": ""Measuring Post"", ""aspect"": ""Ember"",
      ""hp"": 900000000, ""attack"": 1, ""defense"": 0, ""speed"": 1, ""critChance"": 0, ""critDamage"": 1.0,
      ""basic"": { ""id"": ""number_dummy_poke"", ""name"": ""Nudge"",
        ""effects"": [ { ""kind"": ""Damage"", ""target"": ""FrontEnemy"", ""power"": 0.01 } ] } }
  ]
}";
            string campaign = @"{
  ""chapters"": [ { ""id"": ""number_ch"", ""name"": ""Measure"", ""stages"": [
    { ""id"": ""number_arena"", ""index"": 1, ""enemies"": [
      { ""id"": ""number_dummy"", ""level"": 1 } ] } ] } ]
}";
            ContentDatabase db = ContentDatabase.Load(heroes, enemies, campaign, balance);
            if (db.Warnings.Count == 0) return db;
            for (int i = 0; i < db.Warnings.Count; i++)
                Line("  [FAIL] ability-number arena warning: " + db.Warnings[i]);
            return null;
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// The body a heal or a ward is measured on, and the reason it has to exist.
        ///
        /// A Heal event carries what was actually restored, not what was offered - the simulation
        /// caps it at the recipient's missing HP - so a heal measured on a hero standing at full
        /// health reads zero and proves nothing. This ally opens the fight by taking 99% of its
        /// own current HP off itself, out of a very large pool, so every heal in the roster lands
        /// whole and the number in the event is the number the ability was worth.
        ///
        /// It is also the lowest-HP ally by RATIO, which is what LowestHpAlly reads, so every
        /// probe aims at it and never at the hero being measured.
        ///
        /// Loaded from its own database and handed over on the HeroInstance, exactly as the sweep
        /// fillers are, so it never enters heroes.json and cannot be summoned or counted.
        /// </summary>
        private static List<HeroDef> BuildAbilityNumberPatient()
        {
            string heroes = @"{
  ""heroes"": [
    { ""id"": ""number_patient"", ""name"": ""Patient"", ""aspect"": ""Ember"", ""role"": ""Bulwark"",
      ""stars"": 3, ""startingHero"": true,
      ""hp"": 400000000, ""attack"": 1, ""defense"": 0, ""speed"": 2, ""critChance"": 0, ""critDamage"": 1.0,
      ""growth"": 0, ""startEnergy"": 0,
      ""passive"": { ""id"": ""number_patient_open"", ""name"": ""Opened Wound"", ""trigger"": ""BattleStart"",
        ""oncePerBattle"": true,
        ""effects"": [ { ""kind"": ""Damage"", ""target"": ""Self"", ""power"": 0,
                       ""targetCurrentHpRatio"": 0.99 } ] },
      ""basic"": { ""id"": ""number_patient_basic"", ""name"": ""Wait"",
        ""effects"": [ { ""kind"": ""Damage"", ""target"": ""FrontEnemy"", ""power"": 0.01 } ] } }
  ]
}";
            ContentDatabase db = ContentDatabase.Load(heroes, "{\"enemies\":[]}", "{\"chapters\":[]}", "{}");
            HeroDef patient = db.GetHero("number_patient");
            if (patient == null) return null;
            List<HeroDef> list = new List<HeroDef>();
            list.Add(patient);
            return list;
        }

        private static void WriteHeroSweepBaseline(string path, List<string> lines)
        {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            sb.Append("# Emberfall authored-hero sweep baseline.\r\n");
            sb.Append("#\r\n");
            sb.Append("# One line per authored hero per seed: <heroId>|<seed>|<fnv1a-64 of the battle>.\r\n");
            sb.Append("# The hash folds event type, source, target, value and status over the whole\r\n");
            sb.Append("# event stream, plus the outcome and the round count.\r\n");
            sb.Append("#\r\n");
            sb.Append("# <seed> is 1..5, a label. The seed the battle actually runs at is derived from\r\n");
            sb.Append("# the hero ID, so authoring a hero re-seeds nobody and moves no existing line.\r\n");
            sb.Append("#\r\n");
            sb.Append("# A MOVED LINE IS A BEHAVIOUR CHANGE. It needs a stated reason in the commit that\r\n");
            sb.Append("# moves it - which hero, which clause, and why the new behaviour is the right one.\r\n");
            sb.Append("# Regenerating to make a surprise go away is how a guard stops guarding.\r\n");
            sb.Append("#\r\n");
            sb.Append("# Regenerate deliberately:  EMBERFALL_REGEN_HERO_SWEEP=1  pwsh Tools/build-check.ps1\r\n");
            for (int i = 0; i < lines.Count; i++) sb.Append(lines[i]).Append("\r\n");
            File.WriteAllText(path, sb.ToString());
        }

        private static void CompareHeroSweepBaseline(string path, List<string> lines)
        {
            Dictionary<string, string> baseline = new Dictionary<string, string>();
            string[] raw = File.ReadAllLines(path);
            for (int i = 0; i < raw.Length; i++)
            {
                string t = raw[i].Trim();
                if (t.Length == 0 || t[0] == '#') continue;
                int cut = t.LastIndexOf('|');
                if (cut <= 0) continue;
                baseline[t.Substring(0, cut)] = t.Substring(cut + 1);
            }

            List<string> moved = new List<string>();
            List<string> added = new List<string>();
            Dictionary<string, bool> seen = new Dictionary<string, bool>();
            for (int i = 0; i < lines.Count; i++)
            {
                int cut = lines[i].LastIndexOf('|');
                string key = lines[i].Substring(0, cut);
                string hash = lines[i].Substring(cut + 1);
                seen[key] = true;
                string was;
                if (!baseline.TryGetValue(key, out was)) { added.Add(key); continue; }
                if (was != hash) moved.Add(key);
            }
            List<string> gone = new List<string>();
            foreach (KeyValuePair<string, string> kv in baseline)
                if (!seen.ContainsKey(kv.Key)) gone.Add(kv.Key);

            Check(moved.Count == 0,
                  moved.Count == 0
                    ? "all " + lines.Count + " sweep fingerprints match " + HeroSweepBaselineFile
                      + " - no authored kit's behaviour moved"
                    : moved.Count + " of " + lines.Count + " sweep fingerprints MOVED: "
                      + string.Join(", ", moved.ToArray())
                      + " - each is a behaviour change in that hero's kit and needs a stated reason in"
                      + " the commit that moves it, NOT a regenerate");
            Check(added.Count == 0,
                  added.Count == 0
                    ? "no sweep entry is missing from the baseline"
                    : added.Count + " sweep entr(ies) are not in the baseline (a hero was authored or"
                      + " the seed count changed): " + string.Join(", ", added.ToArray()));
            Check(gone.Count == 0,
                  gone.Count == 0
                    ? "no baseline entry has lost its hero"
                    : gone.Count + " baseline entr(ies) no longer resolve to an authored hero: "
                      + string.Join(", ", gone.ToArray()));
        }

        /// <summary>FNV-1a over the battle, folding the same fields the stage fingerprint does plus
        /// the status kind, so a line is a short hash rather than a whole event stream.</summary>
        /// <summary>
        /// The seed a hero's line is run at, KEYED TO THE HERO'S ID rather than to its position in
        /// the sorted list.
        ///
        /// IT WAS `4400000 + index * 97 + seed`, AND THAT MADE THE GUARD LIE. The list is sorted by
        /// id, so authoring a hero shifts the index of every hero alphabetically after it, which
        /// re-seeds their battles and moves their hashes - while the file's own header says a moved
        /// line IS a behaviour change. Authoring these four moved twenty lines belonging to Rheia,
        /// Seraphine, Velis and Zella, whose kits nobody had touched. Measured, not assumed: with
        /// the four still placeholders the same engine change moved none of those twenty.
        ///
        /// A guard that cries wolf on every insertion is a guard that gets regenerated, and a
        /// baseline regenerated to quiet a surprise has stopped guarding. Keyed to the id, a hero's
        /// seeds are its own for as long as it keeps its id, and inserting a hero moves nobody.
        ///
        /// The cost is paid once: every line in the baseline moves on the pass that makes this
        /// change, because every seed changes. That is the last time an insertion moves one.
        ///
        /// Collisions between two heroes' seeds are harmless. Nothing compares two heroes' battles
        /// for equality of seed - the per-seed collision check compares FINGERPRINTS, and two heroes
        /// have always run at different seeds under the old formula too.
        /// </summary>
        private static int HeroSweepSeed(string heroId, int seed)
        {
            ulong hash = 14695981039346656037UL;
            for (int i = 0; i < heroId.Length; i++)
            {
                hash ^= heroId[i];
                hash *= 1099511628211UL;
            }
            return 4400000 + (int)(hash % 3000000UL) + seed;
        }

        /// <summary>Enemies that died in this battle. The straggler is meant to be one of them in
        /// every run; a zero here is the arena having gone blind to defeats again.</summary>
        /// <summary>
        /// NO ABILITY LANDS ITS DAMAGE ON ITS OWN CASTER unless it says Self.
        ///
        /// Two shipped abilities did, and neither was visible to anything else in this harness,
        /// because a hero hitting itself is a legal fight that ends, fingerprints and balances like
        /// any other:
        ///
        ///   - Nysera's False Target answers her Link turning a blow away from her, and aimed its
        ///     extra damage at BroadcastVictim - the body the share came OFF, which for a Deflect
        ///     is always Nysera. It struck her every time it fired.
        ///   - Darian's Hidden Strength sits on Darian and names the highest-Attack enemy as the
        ///     body its payout strikes. The damage branch of a Ledger's auto-release ignored the
        ///     named target and struck the holder, which is Darian.
        ///
        /// The rule is read off the record, which is where the screen reads it: a Damage event whose
        /// source and target are the same unit. Two shapes are EXPLICITLY self-aimed and pass:
        /// a Damage effect authored with target Self on an ability of that name (the sweep
        /// straggler's Bleeding Out is one, and is what proves this check can tell the two apart),
        /// and the donor half of an HpTransfer, whose whole meaning is that the caster pays HP
        /// (emitted with the "transfer" vfx). A damage-over-time tick is a status resolving, not an
        /// ability aiming, and is not read here.
        ///
        /// Run over the sweep arena for every hero with no talent and with each talent in turn, at
        /// the sweep's own seeds, because a talent is where both defects lived or were reached. And
        /// the two cases that motivated it are asserted to have FIRED and landed elsewhere, so a
        /// repair that merely silenced them could not pass for one that aimed them.
        /// </summary>
        private static void CheckNoAbilityHitsItsOwnCaster(ContentDatabase live)
        {
            Line("NO ABILITY HITS ITS OWN CASTER (every hero, every talent, the sweep arena)");

            ContentDatabase arena = BuildHeroSweepArena(live);
            List<HeroDef> fillers = BuildHeroSweepFillers();
            StageDef stage = arena == null ? null : arena.GetStage("sweep_arena");
            if (arena == null || fillers == null || stage == null)
            {
                Check(false, "the sweep arena, its fillers and its stage were built");
                Line("");
                return;
            }

            List<HeroDef> heroes = new List<HeroDef>(live.Heroes);
            heroes.Sort(delegate (HeroDef a, HeroDef b) { return string.CompareOrdinal(a.Id, b.Id); });

            Dictionary<string, int> violations = new Dictionary<string, int>();
            int battles = 0, allowedSelf = 0, falseTargetElsewhere = 0, hiddenStrengthElsewhere = 0;
            for (int h = 0; h < heroes.Count; h++)
            {
                HeroDef def = heroes[h];
                for (int talent = 0; talent <= 3; talent++)
                {
                    if (talent > 0 && def.TalentAt(talent) == null) continue;
                    for (int s = 1; s <= HeroSweepSeeds; s++)
                    {
                        List<HeroInstance> team = new List<HeroInstance>();
                        HeroInstance subject = MakeInstance(def, 40);
                        subject.SelectedTalent = talent;
                        team.Add(subject);
                        for (int f = 0; f < fillers.Count; f++) team.Add(MakeInstance(fillers[f], 40));
                        BattleResult r = RunWith(arena, stage, HeroSweepSeed(def.Id, s), team);
                        if (r == null) continue;
                        battles++;

                        for (int i = 0; i < r.Events.Count; i++)
                        {
                            BattleEvent e = r.Events[i];
                            if (e.Type != BattleEventType.Damage || e.SourceId < 0) continue;
                            UnitSnapshot src = r.FindUnit(e.SourceId);
                            if (src == null) continue;

                            if (e.TargetId != e.SourceId)
                            {
                                if (src.DefId == "nysera_the_missing_arrow" && e.AbilityName == "False Target")
                                    falseTargetElsewhere++;
                                if (src.DefId == "darian_the_hidden_bulwark" && e.AbilityName == "Ledger")
                                    hiddenStrengthElsewhere++;
                                continue;
                            }
                            if (e.Status != StatusKind.None) continue;          // a DoT tick
                            if (e.Vfx == "transfer") { allowedSelf++; continue; }  // HpTransfer's donor
                            UnitDef owner = (UnitDef)arena.GetHero(src.DefId) ?? arena.GetEnemy(src.DefId);
                            if (NamesSelfDamage(owner, e.AbilityName)) { allowedSelf++; continue; }

                            string key = src.DefId + " / " + (string.IsNullOrEmpty(e.AbilityName) ? "(unnamed)" : e.AbilityName)
                                         + (talent > 0 ? " [talent " + RomanOf(talent) + "]" : "");
                            int n;
                            violations.TryGetValue(key, out n);
                            violations[key] = n + 1;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }

            List<string> listed = new List<string>();
            foreach (KeyValuePair<string, int> kv in violations) listed.Add(kv.Key + " x" + kv.Value);
            listed.Sort(string.CompareOrdinal);
            Check(violations.Count == 0,
                  violations.Count == 0
                    ? "no ability landed damage on its own caster in " + battles + " battles, unless it names Self ("
                      + allowedSelf + " explicit self-hits passed)"
                    : violations.Count + " ability/abilities landed damage on their own caster without naming Self: "
                      + string.Join(", ", listed.ToArray()));
            Check(allowedSelf > 0,
                  "the check can tell an explicit self-hit from a defect - it passed " + allowedSelf
                  + " (the straggler's Bleeding Out and HpTransfer donors), so a zero above is a reading, not a blind spot");
            Check(falseTargetElsewhere > 0,
                  "Nysera's False Target fired and struck a body other than Nysera " + falseTargetElsewhere
                  + " time(s) - the body her Link turned the blow onto");
            Check(hiddenStrengthElsewhere > 0,
                  "Darian's Hidden Strength paid out onto a body other than Darian " + hiddenStrengthElsewhere
                  + " time(s) - the highest-Attack enemy it names");
            Line("");
        }

        /// <summary>Whether the unit authors, on an ability of that name, a Damage effect aimed at
        /// Self. Names rather than ids because that is what a Damage event carries.</summary>
        private static bool NamesSelfDamage(UnitDef def, string abilityName)
        {
            if (def == null || string.IsNullOrEmpty(abilityName)) return false;
            List<List<EffectDef>> lists = new List<List<EffectDef>>();
            if (def.BasicAttack != null && def.BasicAttack.Name == abilityName) lists.Add(def.BasicAttack.Effects);
            if (def.Active != null && def.Active.Name == abilityName) lists.Add(def.Active.Effects);
            for (int i = 0; i < def.Passives.Count; i++)
                if (def.Passives[i] != null && def.Passives[i].Name == abilityName) lists.Add(def.Passives[i].Effects);
            HeroDef hero = def as HeroDef;
            if (hero != null)
                for (int i = 0; i < hero.Talents.Count; i++)
                    if (hero.Talents[i] != null && hero.Talents[i].Name == abilityName) lists.Add(hero.Talents[i].Effects);
            for (int l = 0; l < lists.Count; l++)
                for (int i = 0; i < lists[l].Count; i++)
                    if (lists[l][i].Kind == EffectKind.Damage && lists[l][i].Target == TargetMode.Self) return true;
            return false;
        }

        private static int EnemyDeaths(BattleResult r)
        {
            int n = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < r.Events.Count; i++)
            {
                if (r.Events[i].Type != BattleEventType.Death) continue;
                for (int u = 0; u < r.Units.Count; u++)
                    if (r.Units[u].Id == r.Events[i].TargetId && !r.Units[u].IsPlayer) { n++; break; }
            }
            return n;
        }

        private static string HeroSweepHash(BattleResult r)
        {
            ulong hash = 14695981039346656037UL;
            HeroSweepMix(ref hash, r.Victory ? 1 : 0);
            HeroSweepMix(ref hash, r.Rounds);
            HeroSweepMix(ref hash, r.Events.Count);
            for (int i = 0; i < r.Events.Count; i++)
            {
                BattleEvent e = r.Events[i];
                HeroSweepMix(ref hash, (int)e.Type);
                HeroSweepMix(ref hash, e.SourceId);
                HeroSweepMix(ref hash, e.TargetId);
                HeroSweepMix(ref hash, e.Value);
                HeroSweepMix(ref hash, (int)e.Status);
            }
            return hash.ToString("x16");
        }

        private static void HeroSweepMix(ref ulong hash, int v)
        {
            for (int b = 0; b < 4; b++)
            {
                hash ^= (byte)(v >> (b * 8));
                hash *= 1099511628211UL;
            }
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// The arena: the REAL heroes and the REAL balance, against a fixed synthetic enemy line on
        /// a fixed synthetic stage.
        ///
        /// Real balance rather than a zero-variance one, which is the opposite of what the small
        /// mechanic arenas do and is right here for the opposite reason. Those isolate one rule and
        /// want no noise; this wants maximum SENSITIVITY - with damage and speed variance live,
        /// every hit draws from the stream, so a change that merely shifts RNG consumption moves the
        /// hash instead of hiding inside a zeroed term.
        ///
        /// The enemy line is five bodies chosen to give reactive kits something to react to: a
        /// heavy hitter that aims at the weakest ally, a healer, a Burn applier, a self-buffer and
        /// a Taunt holder. Between them they raise the damage, heal, DoT, buff-gained and anchor
        /// paths every round without any of it depending on which hero is being measured.
        ///
        /// THE WARDEN SMOULDERS, and that is a repair rather than flavour. The first shape of this
        /// arena left every enemy carrying identical DoT, so a pick that reads CARRIED DoT and a
        /// pick that falls through to the front body resolved to the same enemy - and the sweep
        /// measurably failed to notice Cyrra's targeting being corrected from one to the other.
        /// The back body now carries a long, shallow self-Burn nobody else has, so the enemy with
        /// the most pending DoT is a specific and distant body. It costs it about 2000 HP of its
        /// 300000 across a whole fight, so it perturbs the line barely at all while making a whole
        /// axis of targeting behaviour visible.
        ///
        /// A SIXTH BODY DIES, and that is the third repair to this arena and the same kind of
        /// repair as the other two. The five bodies above all have 180,000 HP or more and none of
        /// them ever died, so `OnEnemyDefeated` was never raised in a single one of the 145 runs -
        /// and the sweep was blind to every clause that answers a defeat. Three shipped passives
        /// live there: Zella's `Nothing Stays Settled`, Sylven's `Persistent Edge` and Vaelis's
        /// `Opening Moves`. Zella's had been inert in the engine for two batches and the sweep had
        /// no opinion about the repair, because it had never once reached the rule.
        ///
        /// IT IS FIRST IN THE LINE AND THE LOWEST HP IN IT, which is what makes it the body the
        /// marking clauses find. `FrontEnemy` and `LowestHpEnemy` are the two picks the roster's
        /// defeat clauses build on - Zella burns the front body, Vaelis binds the lowest-HP one -
        /// so a corpse that carried neither would raise the trigger and move nothing, which is a
        /// worse kind of blindness than not raising it at all.
        ///
        /// ITS HP IS THE WHOLE OF THE TUNING. Too low and it dies to the subject's opening blow,
        /// before anything has been put on it, and the corpse is bare; too high and the slowest
        /// killers in the roster never finish it. The number below is the one that leaves it
        /// standing after the first hero turn and dead inside the fight for every authored kit -
        /// measured across all 29 by the death count the exercise report prints.
        ///
        /// Once it falls the line is the five bodies it always was, so everything the paragraphs
        /// below describe still holds for the rest of the fight.
        ///
        /// ITS TAUNT LASTS TWO ROUNDS AND IS NEVER RENEWED, which is the other half of the same
        /// repair and the larger one. Re-applied every turn it made the warden a PERMANENT anchor,
        /// and the anchor overrides every single-enemy target mode there is - so every hero that
        /// picks one enemy resolved onto the warden regardless of what its kit asked for, and the
        /// sweep was blind to the whole of single-target targeting. Measured, not suspected: with
        /// the permanent Taunt standing, correcting Cyrra's active from one target mode to another
        /// moved none of her lines. Two anchored rounds still exercise the anchor path; the rest
        /// of the fight lets a kit aim where it says it aims.
        /// </summary>
        private static ContentDatabase BuildHeroSweepArena(ContentDatabase live)
        {
            string dir = FindContentDirForSweep();
            if (dir == null) return null;
            string heroes, balance;
            try
            {
                heroes = File.ReadAllText(Path.Combine(dir, "heroes.json"));
                balance = File.ReadAllText(Path.Combine(dir, "balance.json"));
            }
            catch (Exception) { return null; }

            string enemies = @"{
  ""enemies"": [
